---
// The support text: plans, prices, promises and how to buy. One source for the page at
// provimux.providers.dev/support/ and the document /api/v1/docs/support of the providers.dev API.
interface Props {
  /** Where "Provimux" in the first sentence links to. */
  home: string;
}
const { home } = Astro.props;
const provimux = "https://github.com/ngvoicu/provimux";
const cliproxyapi = "https://github.com/router-for-me/CLIProxyAPI";
const email = "office@providers.dev";
const ask = (plan?: string) => `mailto:${email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(plan ? `Provimux support: ${plan}` : "Provimux support")}`;
---

<div class="prose">
  <p>
    <a href={home}>Provimux</a> is a self-hosted AI gateway for you or your whole team: your subscriptions and API keys behind one endpoint, open to everyone you choose,
    with the prompt cache kept in use and the account behind every answer shown. It is an open-source (MIT) distribution of <a href={cliproxyapi}>CLIProxyAPI</a> by
    router-for-me, and runs on your own machine or on a server your team shares.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plans">Plans</h2>
  <p>Prices are approximate, per month, in US dollars. Prices in euros on request. Prices are before VAT; VAT is added where it applies.</p>
</div>

<div class="plans">
  <section class="plan" aria-labelledby="plan-community">
    <h3 id="plan-community">Community</h3>
    <p class="plan-price">Free</p>
    <ul>
      <li>GitHub issues and discussions, in public</li>
      <li>Best effort: no reply time</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="plan-action"><a class="button" href={`${provimux}/issues`}>Open an issue</a></p>
  </section>
  <section class="plan" aria-labelledby="plan-team">
    <h3 id="plan-team">Team</h3>
    <p class="plan-price">about $149 <span>/ month</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>First reply within 3 business days</li>
      <li>1 hour of help a month</li>
      <li>A private email channel</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="plan-action"><a class="button primary" href={ask("Team")}>Ask for Team</a></p>
  </section>
  <section class="plan" aria-labelledby="plan-business">
    <h3 id="plan-business">Business</h3>
    <p class="plan-price">about $499 <span>/ month</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>First reply within 2 business days</li>
      <li>3 hours of help a month</li>
      <li>An onboarding call</li>
      <li>A vendor pack on request: company details, VAT number, tax forms</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="plan-action"><a class="button primary" href={ask("Business")}>Ask for Business</a></p>
  </section>
  <section class="plan" aria-labelledby="plan-priority">
    <h3 id="plan-priority">Priority</h3>
    <p class="plan-price">about $1,499 <span>/ month, billed yearly</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>First reply the next business day</li>
      <li>8 hours of help a month</li>
      <li>Everything in Business</li>
      <li>At most three customers at a time: ask whether a place is free</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="plan-action"><a class="button primary" href={ask("Priority")}>Ask for Priority</a></p>
  </section>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <p>
    <strong>Extra work</strong>, beyond a plan's hours or without a plan: $150 to $200 an hour, quoted before it starts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="promises">What a plan promises</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A first reply, not a fix.</strong> The days a plan names are the time to a first reply from the maintainer. Many questions are settled in that reply. A bug can take
      longer, and no date for a fix is promised.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Business days.</strong> Monday to Friday, 09:00 to 17:00 Central European Time (CET, and CEST in summer). A request sent outside those hours counts from the next
      business morning. Holidays are announced to customers in advance and pause the clock.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One maintainer.</strong> Provimux is maintained by one person, working with AI agents. That person reads every support request and is responsible for every reply.
      There is no 24/7 cover, and no uptime promise for software you host yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If support ends.</strong> Should providers.dev stop offering support, the unused part of what you paid is refunded, prorated. The code stays MIT: you can keep running
      it, or fork it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Security fixes, early.</strong> Customers on a paid plan hear about a security fix before it is announced in public.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="covered">What support covers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Setup and upgrades.</strong> Installing Provimux, and moving to a new release.</li>
    <li><strong>A review of your configuration.</strong> Accounts, priorities, model routes and cache settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Moving from CLIProxyAPI.</strong> Taking an existing CLIProxyAPI setup over to Provimux.</li>
    <li><strong>Debugging.</strong> A request that fails, a route that is paused, a prompt cache that is not read.</li>
    <li><strong>Priority on bugs.</strong> A bug you report goes ahead of the public queue. The fix is published in the open code, for everyone.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>What it does not cover</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Access to models or accounts.</strong> providers.dev does not sell, pool, share or resell access to any model or account. You bring your own API keys or cloud
      accounts (for example Vertex), billed to your company under its own agreement with the provider.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Running Provimux for you.</strong> It runs on your machines, and nothing goes through providers.dev.</li>
    <li><strong>One person's subscription login shared by a team.</strong> For a company, use API keys and cloud accounts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="buy">How to buy</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Write to <a href={ask()}>{email}</a> with the plan, your company's name and country, and whether you need a vendor pack. Each plan above has a button that starts
      the email.
    </li>
    <li>
      You get an invoice, paid by bank transfer or by card (a Stripe invoice). Invoices come from a VAT-registered business in Romania. An EU business with a VAT number is
      invoiced under the reverse charge.
    </li>
    <li>Support starts when the first invoice is paid.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Or sponsor on GitHub: the Team and Business plans are also on <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/ngvoicu">GitHub Sponsors</a> as the $149 and $499 monthly tiers.
    Write to <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> after sponsoring so we can set up your support.
  </p>
  <p>The company details, the VAT number and the tax forms are in the vendor pack, sent on request from Business up.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>
  <h3>How fast is a reply?</h3>
  <p>
    Within the business days of your plan: three on Team, two on Business, the next business day on Priority. That is the first reply from the maintainer, not a fix. Community
    questions on GitHub are answered when there is time.
  </p>
  <h3>We are in another time zone.</h3>
  <p>
    Business hours are 09:00 to 17:00 Central European Time, Monday to Friday. For a team on US Eastern time that is about 03:00 to 11:00. Send a request at any hour; it is read
    on the next business day. Calls are booked within those hours.
  </p>
  <h3>What counts as an hour?</h3>
  <p>
    Time spent on your requests: calls, reading your configuration and logs, reproducing a problem, writing the reply. It is counted in quarter hours. Hours left unused do not
    carry over to the next month. Fixing a bug in Provimux itself is not counted: the fix is public.
  </p>
  <h3>Can we cancel?</h3>
  <p>
    Team and Business are monthly: cancel by email at any time, and the plan ends with the month already paid. Priority is billed yearly and runs to the end of the paid year. If
    providers.dev ends support, the unused part is refunded, prorated.
  </p>
  <h3>Do you need our API keys or access to our systems?</h3>
  <p>
    No. We never ask for your API keys, logins or prompts, and never need access to your machines. Send configuration and logs with the keys removed; on a call, you share your
    screen and keep control. Provimux runs on your machines, and nothing goes through providers.dev.
  </p>
</div>
